# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and in the working tree,
# on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference. Two things in Verity depend on it:
#
#   * `gofmt -l .` must print nothing (docs/SPEC.md §12, and the CI gate).
#     gofmt insists on LF, so a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true lists
#     every Go file in the repository as unformatted, and the project's own
#     pre-commit check becomes noise that gets ignored.
#
#   * The golden trace in sim/testdata is compared byte for byte against a
#     buffer the simulator wrote with "\n". If git rewrote that file's line
#     endings on checkout, the comparison would fail on Windows and pass on
#     Linux — a difference between machines, in the one test whose entire
#     purpose is to prove there is no difference between machines.
*               text=auto eol=lf

*.go            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.mod           text eol=lf
*.sum           text eol=lf
*.trace         text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
